-- The `extern` arm of `stdlib_member_defs_ok`'s member scan, kept covered after
-- `t126` had to stop using one (2026-07-30).  An `extern` block declares its
-- functions as members of the enclosing module, so a nested `mod List` with a
-- foreign `length` is a competing definition of `List.length` and the measure
-- alias must be WITHDRAWN — a foreign C `length` may return anything at all.
--
-- The competing declaration lives in a NESTED module here, not the entry
-- module, for a specific reason: `strip_entry_self_qual` rewrites a
-- self-qualified reference to the ENTRY module's own members, extern members
-- included as of 2026-07-30, so an entry-level extern `length` would leave the
-- guard below spelled bare `length` and the alias would never have been
-- consulted at all — the witness would pass whatever the gate did.  A nested
-- module's name is not stripped, so `List.length(ys)` survives to the gate.
--
-- With the alias withdrawn, `List.length(ys) == 0` says nothing about
-- `len(ys)` and the right answer is SILENCE.  Verified by mutation: with
-- `stdlib_member_defs_ok`'s `A.DExtern` arm deleted, this program is rejected
-- with a FALSE `refinement violation: ... (e.g. len(ys) = 0)` on `head(ys)`.
mod App do
  -- R1 stage D: `main`'s `Cap(IO.Foreign)` grant is a direct use in THIS
  -- module, so App declares it too — the nested `mod List`'s own `needs`
  -- covers the extern block, not the outer signature.
  needs IO.Foreign
  mod List do
    needs IO.Foreign

    extern "c" : Cap(IO.Foreign) do
      fn length(xs : List(Int)) : Int = "march_shim_list_length"
    end
  end

  fn head(xs : {List(Int) | len(_) > 0}) : List(Int) do xs end

  fn go(ys : List(Int)) : List(Int) do
    if List.length(ys) == 0 do head(ys) else ys end
  end

  fn main(_cap_foreign : Cap(IO.Foreign)) : List(Int) do go([1]) end
end
